/-
Fmt/M2EngineLR.lean: tests that the programs' own proof of the second toy is read from its bytes and
accepted, and that the plan, the chain values and the 34 intermediate values that the programs print
beside it are those that Lean computes.

The second toy is a test protocol of the programs.

The set of files: tiny parameters with a repeated position. These are the tiny parameters (a size
meant for reading by eye; it reaches no security level) under another key of the prover's coins.
Three of the toy's rounds are named LOC, and each draws a list of positions. At that key the second
of the three draws one position twice. That round comes after a link with a kept message (a message
that the next link sends again).

While it reads, the strict reader tests the form, the proofs of work and every Merkle opening. The
decision, the algebraic part of the check, batches all its rows and makes its only checks of
equations between field elements in the last block of the proximity test, so its verdict alone only
shows whether all its parts agree. The programs therefore print, beside a proof, their plan of the
rounds, the state of the hash chain (from which the challenges are drawn) after every round, and 34
field elements that their verifier computes on its way. (What the decision tests before the last
block: the head of Fmt/M2Dec.lean, under 1.)

Sources. This file was generated by a program from files in the archive
toy-files-made-by-the-cuts-tools.tgz (sha256
f97d6774317660e9033841ab36ce4e63e376ebeb25542cf6f1f92e8f61644aa1; the archive is not in this
repository), which the programs (the C++ implementation that comes with this Lean code) made for the
second toy (a chain of three links; Fmt/M2Dec.lean says what it is). The data are made-up toy data:
no model and no weights. Taken from the archive: the files .plan.tsv, .chain.tsv, .targets.tsv of
this set. The proof itself is in Fmt/M2ProgLRFiles.lean. The generating program stops unless every
file that the archive's own list of checksums (SUMS) names, the lists among them, has the sha256
given there. The file imports no Mathlib.

1. What the file contains. Tests, and one helper, `rhos`. How the file is read:
   The description (what a verifier knows before it sees a file: label, parameter bytes, statement,
     rounds) is `Theirs.specTiny` (Fmt/M2Theirs.lean; `S` below).
   The strict reader is `wholeChainF` (Fmt/Rules8Fast.lean), which is proved equal to `wholeChain`
     of Fmt/Rules8.lean at the format's hash. It is this library's rendering of the format page's
     verifier up to its decision.
   The decision is `Fmt.M2.dec` (Fmt/M2All.lean). The values are `Fmt.M2.values`, in the same file.

2. What is checked when the file compiles (#guard). The parameters and the public file are those of
   Fmt/M2Theirs.lean. The programs' table of the plan equals the description's rounds, line for line
   (name; kind, bytes of the prover's message, roots, answers, elements drawn, how many of these
   must not be 0, positions, logarithm of their range, matrix entries drawn, bits of work, rows
   batched, the most words of the verifier's message, the blocks for these words). The file's first
   32 bytes are the chain's random bytes as the programs print them. The digest of the parameters,
   the run value and the first chain value equal the printed ones. For every round: the nonce, the
   elements drawn, the positions drawn and the chain value after the round equal the programs' list.
   The proof is accepted. All 34 values equal the programs', place for place.

3. Theorems. None in this file.

4. What is not claimed.
   No theorem. The tests (#guard) are evaluations that Lean makes when the file compiles. They speak
     of these bytes and of nothing else.
   No statement on soundness follows from these tests.
   Not compared with the programs' lists: a round's message and its first hash call as printed there
     (the message is the file's own bytes), and their lists of fields, queries, leaves, vertices and
     codes.
   Nothing is claimed here of the altered copies (see Fmt/M2EngineLRw.lean) or of the compiled
     verifier (see Run/M2Engine*.lean).

Terms: lean/GLOSSARY.md.
-/
